A1. The fat man's burden. ~ REST
A5. Short pants. ~ GASPS
A10. A seagoing backslider. ~ CRAB
A14. A dental asset. ~ ACHE
A15. When this is broken it takes a fine to patch it. ~ LIMIT
A16. The fellow they sing for in the talkies. ~ HERO
A17. A weather report. ~ THUNDER
A19. Last name of all crack flyers. ~ LIMITED
A21. Lit up. ~ SODDEN
A22. Monopolist in the tire business. ~ BORE
A23. A light beam overhead. ~ RAY
A24. An old Spanish customer. ~ DON
A25. An "i" blacker. ~ DOT
A26. Nothing doing in Scotland. ~ NAE
A28. This is never closed. ~ OPEN
A30. This marks the spot. ~ CROSS
A32. An inveterate night smoker. ~ LAMP
A35. The cat's pajamas. ~ FUR
A36. Pal. ~ CHUM
A37. A regular church-goer. ~ PASTOR
A39. This represents France. ~ FR
A40. Earliest harbinger of spring. ~ POEM
A41. This is almighty for Mexican capitalists. ~ PESO
A42. A flighty partnership that recently became a merger. ~ WE
A43. Attempts. ~ ESSAYS
A45. From last accounts this was still missing. ~ LINK
A46. One thing the Three Blind Mice didn't do. ~ SEE
A47. The car license that is a back number. ~ REAR
A48. A traffic problem. ~ SPEED
A50. A fire-brand. ~ BURN
A51. The guy who fixes the racehorses. ~ VET
A53. Poetic contraction. ~ OER
A54. Swedish verb. ~ BAN
A55. That cold, mirthless laugh (brrrr!). ~ AHA
A57. A sure cure for baldness. ~ HAIR
A59. A suburban weed patch. ~ GARDEN
A62. A few pointers for hot Mexicans. ~ DAGGERS
A64. The dolls that get all the breaks. ~ FRAGILE
A65. Space. ~ AREA
A66. Here's where Japan keeps up a 100% coolie system. ~ KOREA
A68. What people keep busy at along Easy Street. ~ EASE
A69. A sailor cleans up here three times a day. ~ MESS
A70. A cap for the mountains. ~ SNOWY
A71. What they'll go for a ride on this winter in Chicago. ~ SLED

D1. In the old days these made the women high hat. ~ RATS
D2. An automatic repeater. ~ ECHO
D3. Dora thinks this is a thing on a window, but it's only a thrill to us. ~ SHUDDER
D4. This is not good for a hard pull; it would be too much of a strain. ~ TENDON
D5. A woodsy cozy spot. ~ GLEN
D6. A breathing space. ~ AIR
D7. When you repent your Saturday night sins (abbr.). ~ SM
D8. Guides to the higher things of life. ~ PILOTS
D9. What every tabloid tries to create. ~ STIR
D10. Greek letter from Chicago. ~ CHI
D11. The wrong way to stage a comeback. ~ RETREAT
D12. More space. ~ AREA
D13. What a chorus girl puts into her work. ~ BODY
D18. Gorilla's hangout. ~ DEN
D20. Western males. ~ MEN
D22. A big real estate racket. ~ BOOM
D25. If this goes to a dance hall it gets a good beating. ~ DRUM
D27. Too. ~ ALSO
D28. Tender. ~ OFFER
D29. A Scotch moth bag. ~ PURSE
D30. A waiting game. ~ CHESS
D31. Tex Guinan's favorite verb. ~ SPEND
D33. The village cut-up. ~ MOWER
D34. What peacocks do to pass the time. ~ PREEN
D36. This kind of girl waits for someone to start something. ~ COY
D38. How to get a kiss from a girl—it never fails. ~ ASK
D40. Cut away coats—of potatoes, etc. ~ PARE
D41. Here's where the sea turns into land. ~ PIER
D44. Wild parties. ~ SAVAGES
D45. A dirty look. ~ LEER
D46. Just an old-timer. ~ SUNDIAL
D49. A slug of this'll make you dead to the world. ~ POISON
D50. The 10-ton trucks of the deep. ~ BARGES
D52. English article. ~ THE
D54. The shepherd's serenade. ~ BAA
D55. The first assistant dishwasher. ~ ADAM
D56. A fast-stepper; but Dora thinks it is something that always gets into your soup at boarding houses. ~ HARE
D58. The first floating population problem (plur.). ~ ARKS
D59. Respectable hair-coloring. ~ GRAY
D60. Otherwise. ~ ELSE
D61. This is a necessity. ~ NEED
D63. This makes the ship of state go. ~ GAS
D64. The number of happy marriages along Broadway. ~ FEW
D67. Fill this in with as much rot as it will hold. ~ RO


